## 5. How the OS Actually Works — The Mechanism

### System Calls — The Bridge
Applications can't directly touch hardware. When a program needs something from hardware (like reading a file), it makes a **system call** — a formal request to the kernel.

```
Application wants to read a file
        ↓
   System Call (e.g., read())
        ↓
     Kernel processes request
        ↓
   Kernel talks to disk hardware
        ↓
   Data returned to Application
```

This request-response pattern happens **constantly**, thousands of times per second, even for simple tasks.

### User Mode vs Kernel Mode
- **User Mode**: Where applications run. Restricted — can't directly access hardware or memory of other processes.
- **Kernel Mode**: Where the OS core runs. Full, unrestricted access to hardware.

This separation exists purely for **safety** — if applications could directly control hardware, one buggy or malicious program could crash the entire system or access another program's private data.

### Context Switching
Since a CPU can technically only execute one instruction stream at a time (per core), the OS creates the *illusion* of multitasking by rapidly switching between processes — giving each one a tiny slice of time (milliseconds), then switching to the next. This happens so fast that it feels simultaneous to us.

